### 2. What You Can Deduct (Above-the-Line & Business)
Even if you take the standard deduction, you can still claim certain "above-the-line" deductions and business expenses:
*   **Student Loan Interest:** You can deduct up to a maximum of **$2,500** per year in student loan interest (even though you paid $4,400). Because your combined income is well below the 2024 phase-out threshold for married couples (which starts at $155,000), you qualify for the full $2,500 deduction. This will directly lower your Adjusted Gross Income (AGI).
*   **Freelance Business Expenses:** If the $950 in unreimbursed expenses was for your *freelance data analysis work* (and not your W-2 job), you can deduct that directly against your $14,600 freelance income.

### 3. How to Treat the Home Office
You can claim a home office deduction, but **only against your freelance income**, not your W-2 income. Because the space (110 sq ft) is used exclusively for business, it qualifies. You have two ways to calculate this:
*   **Simplified Method:** 110 sq ft × $5 per sq ft = **$550 deduction**.
*   **Actual Expenses Method:** You calculate the percentage of your home used for business (110 sq ft ÷ 780 sq ft = **14.1%**). You pay $1,600/month in rent ($19,200/year). 14.1% of your annual rent is **$2,707**. You can also add 14.1% of your renter's insurance, utilities, and internet bills. 
*   **Verdict:** The **Actual Expenses Method** will give you a significantly higher deduction (at least $2,707 vs. $550) and will substantially lower the self-employment tax you owe on your freelance income.

### 4. Forms You Will Need
When you file, you will need the following forms:
